; AVR GPIO blink — ATtiny1634 (classic tinyAVR).
;
; Stage 14 AVR-4 (design/stage14/avr-targets.md §5). Drives PB0 (Port B, pin 0)
; as an output and toggles it forever with a busy-wait delay — the "hello world"
; of a microcontroller. No timers/interrupts (that is AVR-5/AVR-6); a plain
; counting loop is the correct v1 scope.
;
; Build + link for the ATtiny1634 (a real link via the avr-gcc driver, AVR-3):
;   nucleusc --target=avr --mcpu=attiny1634 examples/avr-blink.nuc -o blink.elf
;
; `(exclude-prelude)`: the prelude auto-emits the intern/arena/node runtime,
; which references host-only libc (`perror`/`malloc`) absent from freestanding
; avr-libc, so it would fail to link (AVR-1/AVR-3). This program needs none of
; it — only the AVR MMIO helpers and the device register file, both of which are
; likewise prelude-free. It also avoids function *values* (an addrspace(1)
; verifier error on AVR, ground truth §1.4); every call here is direct.

(exclude-prelude)

(import-use "lib/avr.nuc")
(import-use "lib/avr/attiny1634.nuc")

; Crude busy-wait. A counting loop, no timer. The exact iteration count is not
; behaviourally verified (the ATtiny1634 is not simavr-simulatable); it exists to
; make the toggle visible on real hardware.
(defn delay ():void
  (let (:volatile i:usize 0)
    (while (< i 20000)
      (inc! i))))

(defn main ():i32
  ; PB0 as output: set its DDRB bit.
  (reg8-set-bit! DDRB 0)
  (while true
    (reg8-toggle-bit! PORTB 0)
    (delay))
  (return 0))
